he molecule  this group is tert-butyl dimethyl shylo  chloride and is sometimes abbreviated  tbdms chloride or more commonly TBS  chloride so we get this whole big group  on here the group is based stable the  base in this reaction is an amine base  so it just takes that proton off after  the TBS group is on the alcohol and a  commonly used base in this reaction is a  Mitte soul now we don't usually draw  this whole group on here let me just  show you how we would abbreviate this  structure now that this oxygen contains  a base stable protecting group we can go  ahead and form a grignard here and  attack this Epoque side now if this  workup is acidic that might knock off  our TB s group right then and so we  might kick off the TB s and free up our  alcohol however we're gonna say it did  not here and i want to show you the  standard conditions for removing a TB s  group which is a fluoride compound  called t bath this is a fluoride salt  tetra butyl ammonium fluoride and it  looks like this so the f minus of t bath  comes in attack set silicon forming a  complex and then this group is removed  from the molecule let's just summarize  how this met the three criteria of a  protecting group this was pretty easy to  put on we just needed this compound here  in the presence of a base probably a mid  acyl and we were able to put the silicon  group onto our oxygen it was stable when  we formed a grignard over here and use  that to open this up oxide and then it  was easily removed with t bath  so far in this lesson we looked at  protecting groups for aldehydes and  ketones the asset al is a good one we  also looked at how to protect an alcohol  from deprotonation by nucleophiles and  looked at the TBS group another group  that we commonly need to protect is  amines so let's take this third example  we're looking at the alkylation of this  Oh H group so we're adding a methyl or a  ch3 here and we're leaving this nitrogen  unreacted first let's just ignore the  amine over her